---
import BaseHead from '../components/BaseHead.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';

const allPosts = await getCollection('blog', ({ data }) => data.status === 'live');
const reviewCount = allPosts.filter(p => p.data.postType === 'review').length;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<BaseHead title="About — The Brooklyn Reader" description="Sabtain Khan, reading in Brooklyn. Reviews, lists, and recommendations." />
	</head>
	<body>
		<Header />
		<main class="about-page">

			<!-- Hero -->
			<div class="about-hero">
				<div class="shell">
					<div class="avatar-side">
						<div class="avatar-wrap">
							<img src="/sk-notion-face.png" alt="Sabtain Khan" />
						</div>
					</div>
					<div class="bio-side">
						<p class="crumbs">§ About · the human behind The Brooklyn Reader</p>
						<h1>Hi, I'm <em>Sabtain.</em></h1>
						<p class="lede">
							I live in Brooklyn and love to read books, then talk about them with
							anyone who'll sit still long enough.
						</p>
						<div class="sig-strip">
							<span><strong>{reviewCount}</strong> books reviewed</span>
							<span>Brooklyn, NY</span>
							<a href="https://www.goodreads.com/saturdays" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Goodreads ↗</a>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<!-- Body -->
			<div class="about-body">
				<div class="shell">
					<article>
						<p class="intro">
							I've read hundreds of books and I'm not done yet. This site is
							the reading log I wish someone else kept — a place to think out
							loud, point at what's good, and leave a trail for whoever's
							curious enough to follow it.
						</p>
						<p>
							Reviews here aren't recommendations exactly. They're closer to letters to a
							friend who asked, <em>"So, was it any good?"</em> — the kind of answer that
							takes a few paragraphs to land properly.
						</p>
						<p>
							I started my more serious reading journey in 2015 and haven't really stopped.
						</p>

						<h2>How I rate</h2>
						<div class="rating-key">
							<div class="rk-stars">★☆☆☆☆</div>
							<div class="rk-row">
								<strong>Don't recommend</strong>
								Derivative or dishonest. Read the source instead.
							</div>

							<div class="rk-stars">★★☆☆☆</div>
							<div class="rk-row">
								<strong>Readable, but offers little</strong>
								Low literary value; mostly forgettable.
							</div>

							<div class="rk-stars">★★★☆☆</div>
							<div class="rk-row">
								<strong>Recommended, with caveats</strong>
								I enjoyed it; it won't push it on you.
							</div>

							<div class="rk-stars">★★★★☆</div>
							<div class="rk-row">
								<strong>Really recommend</strong>
								I'd go out of my way to suggest it.
							</div>

							<div class="rk-stars">★★★★★</div>
							<div class="rk-row">
								<strong>Recommend to almost everyone</strong>
								Fundamentally different. Timeless.
							</div>
						</div>

						<div class="scoring-callout">
							<div class="lbl">A note on scoring</div>
							To me, 5/5 means 100/100, and 4/5 means 80/100. The space
							between them is brutal. Some 4-star books feel closer to a
							4.25–4.5, but I won't round them up. Same issue at 3-stars.
							The scale doesn't capture the nuance I wish it could, and
							I blame scoring systems like Goodreads which only allow
							whole-star ratings.
						</div>

						<h2>How I review</h2>
						<p>
							I take notes while reading. Then Claude interviews me about the book —
							question by question, pressing until I've said what I actually think. My
							answers become the transcript; the transcript becomes the draft.
						</p>
						<p>
							I try to write the review I'd want to read three years from now — when I've
							forgotten most of it and need something to get me back inside the book.
						</p>

						<h2>The site</h2>
						<p>
							Built in Astro and Tailwind. Set in Newsreader and Geist. The shelves you
							see are real; the recommendations are too.
						</p>
					</article>
				</div>
			</div>

		</main>
		<Footer />
	</body>
</html>

<style>
/* Column order for this page (avatar left, bio right) */
.avatar-side { order: 1; }
.bio-side    { order: 2; }

@media (max-width: 880px) {
	.about-hero .shell {
		grid-template-columns: 1fr;
	}
	.avatar-side { order: 2; }
	.bio-side    { order: 1; }
}
</style>
